  { num: '02', title: 'Ignoring cadre and zone trade-offs when filling preferences', body: 'A home cadre is not automatically the right call, and a different-state cadre is not automatically a downgrade. Decide based on family logistics, language comfort, and career exposure, not assumption.' },
  { num: '03', title: 'Assuming a good rank guarantees your first-preference service', body: 'Allocation is by rank against your submitted preference order, but reserved-category vacancies, prior-cycle carryovers, and how higher-ranked candidates ordered their own preferences all shift the exact cutoff every year.' },
  { num: '04', title: 'Treating field postings as a formality on the way to Delhi', body: 'Sub-district and district postings are where administrative judgment is actually built. Officers who rush through them without engaging seriously tend to struggle more at senior central postings later.' },
  { num: '05', title: 'Not planning finances and family logistics around transfer-heavy early postings', body: 'The first decade of an All India Service career usually means frequent transfers, often to smaller towns. Families who plan for this early adjust far better than those who treat each transfer as a fresh shock.' },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'How does UPSC decide which service you get after clearing CSE?',
    a: 'Service allocation is based on your final rank, your submitted preference order on the Detailed Application Form (DAF), your category, and the number of vacancies in each service that year. UPSC allots strictly by rank against your preference order until every vacancy in every service is filled.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What rank do you need for IAS, IPS, or IFS?',
    a: 'The cutoff moves every year. In the 2025 general-category cycle, IAS closed around rank 78, IFS around rank 115, and IPS around rank 232, though a topper who prefers IPS over IAS can still be allotted IPS. Always check the official rank-wise post list published after results, since vacancies and category-wise reservation change the numbers annually.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you choose your cadre, or the state you serve in?',
    a: 'You submit a ranked cadre preference across five zones (the 26 IAS/IPS/IFoS cadres are grouped into these zones), one cadre chosen from each zone in priority order. Allocation then follows your rank, your preference order, and cadre vacancies, so you influence the outcome but do not fully control it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens if you do not get your preferred service despite clearing the exam?',
    a: 'You are allotted the highest-ranked service on your DAF list for which a vacancy remains at your rank and category. Officers can later apply for inter-service transfer in narrow circumstances, but this is rare, discretionary, and not something to plan a career around.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is the Indian Forest Service (IFoS) part of the same exam as IAS, IPS, and IFS?',
    a: 'No. IFoS candidates clear the Civil Services Preliminary Examination together with CSE aspirants, but then sit a separate Indian Forest Service Main Examination and interview. It is a distinct recruitment track, not allotted through the regular CSE preference and rank process.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How soon can an IAS or IPS officer go on deputation to the central government or abroad?',
    a: 'Central deputation to a ministry typically becomes realistic after a state stint of several years. Deputation to international organisations such as the World Bank, IMF, ADB, or UN agencies is usually possible after around 9 years of service, sometimes earlier in exceptional cases.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What can civil servants do after retirement?',
    a: 'Common paths include corporate board positions, government-relations and policy-consulting roles, joining large consulting firms, writing or academic work, and in a smaller number of cases, entering electoral politics. General administrative experience is valued broadly, though it usually needs to be paired with a clear area of specialisation to compete for top-tier private roles.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is IRS a lower service than IAS or IPS?',
    a: 'IRS is a separate Group A central service, not a lower tier of the same ladder. It typically opens up at a lower rank than IAS, IPS, or IFS because of how DAF preferences and rank cutoffs work, but it carries its own strong career trajectory in tax policy, enforcement, and revenue administration, including national roles at the Central Board of Direct Taxes or Central Board of Indirect Taxes and Customs.',
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="government-jobs"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Career options after UPSC CSE India open up the moment your final rank and service preference are matched against that year's vacancies.</strong>
    IAS, IPS, IFS, IRS, and more than a dozen other Group A central services all recruit through the same exam, but which one you actually get, which state you serve in, and what your day-to-day work looks like for the next three decades depends on rank, preference order, and cadre choices most aspirants only think about after the results are out. Get this decision right and you step onto a genuinely stable, high-authority income path years before a peer who ranks services by reputation and has to correct course later.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Service allocation runs on rank plus your Detailed Application Form (DAF) preference order, not just rank alone — a topper who lists IPS above IAS gets IPS.</li>
      <li>IAS, IFS, IPS, and IRS close in roughly that order by rank each year, but the exact cutoff shifts with vacancies and category-wise reservation.</li>
      <li>Cadre allocation decides which state you actually work in, using a five-zone preference system separate from your service choice.</li>
      <li>Each service has its own multi-decade ladder — field postings first, then state or central leadership roles, then, for a smaller group, international deputation or post-retirement corporate and consulting work.</li>
      <li><strong>Decide your DAF preference order using the actual daily work of each service, not its perceived prestige — you cannot change it once submitted.</strong></li>
      <li>If your rank does not land the service you expected, the same preparation years still built a high-income skill portfolio — analytical writing, policy research, structured communication — that transfers directly into strong non-government careers.</li>
      <li>Clearing UPSC does not remove the trade-offs: hierarchy, transfers, public scrutiny, institutional pressure, and structured pay remain part of the work. Judge each service by the life and autonomy it permits, not only status.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    For a wider view of how government careers compare to private-sector paths, start with <a href="/blog/government-jobs/">Government Jobs</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you are weighing a civil-services path against other options, or want help mapping your specific rank scenario, <a href="/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">structured career guidance built around exactly this kind of decision</a> can walk through your options with you.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="allocation">How service allocation actually works</h2>

  <p>
    Clearing the Civil Services Examination gets you onto a merit list. It does not, by itself, tell you which service you will join.
  </p>

  <p>
    Once the final result is out, UPSC allocates a service to each recommended candidate using three things together: your final rank, your submitted preference order for services on the DAF, and your reservation category. Allocation moves down the merit list and assigns each candidate the highest-ranked service on their own preference list that still has a vacancy for their category.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Service (2025 cycle)</th>
          <th>Vacancies</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {vacancyRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Service"><strong>{row.item}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Vacancies">{row.figure}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Source: <a href="https://www.studyiq.com/articles/upsc-cadre-allocation-policy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">StudyIQ, UPSC Cadre Allocation Policy 2025</a> and the official <a href="https://cseplus.dopt.gov.in/Home/ServiceAllocation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DoPT CSE Candidates' Information Central service allocation page</a>. Verify current-year vacancy figures on the official notification before planning around them.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Where the confusion usually starts</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>Assuming your rank alone decides your service, without your own preference order playing any part.</li>
      <li>Believing IAS is the only "real" outcome and everything else is a fallback.</li>
      <li>Filling the DAF preference list quickly, based on family opinion, instead of researching each service's actual work.</li>
      <li>Not realising the preference order becomes locked early and cannot be edited later.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      None of that tells you what a Deputy Commissioner in the IRS actually does all day, or how soon an IPS officer reaches SP rank. That is the part worth knowing before you rank your choices.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    From the 2025 examination cycle onward, candidates must submit their service and cadre preferences within 10 days of the Preliminary Examination result, and changes are not permitted once submitted. That means the real decision-making window is earlier and shorter than most aspirants expect.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rank-reality">What your rank realistically gets you</h2>

  <p>
    Aspirants often ask "what rank do I need for IAS" as if it were a fixed number. It is not. It moves every year with vacancies, the number of candidates, and category-wise reservation. But the general pattern across recent cycles has stayed consistent.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Service</th>
          <th>Approx. last rank, general category (2025)</th>
          <th>Why it lands here</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {rankRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Service"><strong>{row.service}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Approx. last rank">{row.rank}</td>
            <td data-label="Why it lands here">{row.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Source: <a href="https://pwonlyias.com/last-rank-for-ias-ips-ifs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PWOnlyIAS, Last Rank for IAS, IPS, IFS 2025</a>. These figures shift every year — check the current official rank-wise post list on <a href="https://upsc.gov.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">upsc.gov.in</a> before planning around a specific number.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {worthCards.map((item) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="feature-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A candidate ranked outside the IAS or IPS cutoff has not "failed" the civil services. They have qualified for a genuinely strong career inside IRS or one of the other Group A services, each with its own multi-decade ladder and real authority.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ranking every service by how close it sounds to IAS, instead of by the actual daily work, is the single most common early mistake on the DAF.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="cadre">Cadre allocation: which state you serve in</h2>

  <p>
    Service and cadre are two separate decisions, and both matter. Cadre allocation decides which state or group of states you will actually work in for most of your IAS, IPS, or IFoS career.
  </p>

  <p>
    Under the revised Cadre Allocation Policy, the 26 IAS, IPS, and Indian Forest Service cadres are grouped into five zones. You rank cadre preferences by picking one cadre from each zone, in order of priority, rather than ranking all 26 individually. Allocation then follows your rank, category, your stated order, and cadre-wise vacancies.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Narrow thinking</div>
      <ul>
        {cadreComparison.narrow.map((line) => <li>{line}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Better thinking</div>
      <ul>
        {cadreComparison.better.map((line) => <li>{line}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    IFS officers do not get a state cadre in the same sense — their postings rotate between Indian missions abroad and Ministry of External Affairs headquarters in Delhi, alternating roughly every few years. IRS and most other Group A central services are posted across zones and cities nationally rather than tied to one state cadre.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ias-path">Career trajectory inside the IAS</h2>

  <p>
    An IAS career runs on a predictable ladder of field responsibility first, then central-government policy roles. Timelines below are typical ranges, not fixed guarantees — track record and vacancies shift them for individual officers.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
    {iasSteps.map((item) => (
      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div class="checkpoint__body">
          <div class="checkpoint__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Source: composite of current IAS promotion-timeline reporting including <a href="https://pwonlyias.com/ias-promotion-chart-with-years/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PWOnlyIAS, IAS Promotion Chart</a>. Actual timing depends on cadre, vacancies, and individual service record.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ips-path">Career trajectory inside the IPS</h2>

  <p>
    IPS careers move through police ranks with clearer command responsibility at each stage than most people expect from outside the service.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Rank</th>
          <th>Typical timeline</th>
          <th>What changes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {ipsRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Rank"><strong>{row.rank}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Typical timeline">{row.years}</td>
            <td data-label="What changes">{row.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Source: <a href="https://advocategandhi.com/promotion-structure-in-ips-from-asp-to-dgp/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">promotion-structure reporting on IPS careers</a>. DGP is a function of seniority, empanelment, and state government selection, not an automatic promotion by years served — there is only one DGP per state at a time.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Many IPS officers spend part of their career on central deputation to the CBI, Intelligence Bureau, National Investigation Agency, or Central Armed Police Forces.
    </p>
    <p>
      This national-level exposure is often read as strengthening a case for senior state promotions later, not as a detour from the main career.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ifs-path">Career trajectory inside the IFS</h2>

  <p>
    The Indian Foreign Service is the only one of the three top services where your career alternates between countries rather than districts. Officers typically spend around three years abroad, then two to three years at Ministry of External Affairs headquarters in Delhi, cycling through this pattern repeatedly.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
    {ifsSteps.map((item) => (
      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div class="checkpoint__body">
          <div class="checkpoint__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Source: <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_Foreign_Service" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Indian Foreign Service career-structure overview</a> and current MEA career reporting. Officers may also serve the consulate track (Vice-Consul, Consul, Consul General) instead of the embassy track at various points.
  </p>

  <h2 id="irs-path">IRS and the other Group A central services</h2>

  <p>
    IRS is the largest Group A service by intake, and it has its own complete ladder, separate from IAS or IPS, working under the Ministry of Finance's Department of Revenue.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Grade</th>
          <th>What it involves</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {irsRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Grade"><strong>{row.grade}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What it involves">{row.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Entry-level IRS pay starts near Rs 56,100 a month (Level 10 basic), rising with allowances and promotions to roughly Rs 2,25,000 gross at senior-most positions. Source: <a href="https://vajiramandravi.com/upsc-exam/irs-officer-salary/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Vajiram & Ravi, IRS Officer Salary</a>. Verify current pay-commission figures before planning around them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Beyond IAS, IPS, IFS, and IRS, the same exam feeds more than a dozen other Group A central services, each running its own ministry or department with real authority. A few worth knowing by name:
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Service</th>
          <th>What it does</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {otherGroupARows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Service"><strong>{row.service}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What it does">{row.role}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Source: composite list from <a href="https://byjus.com/free-ias-prep/civil-services-post-one-may-get-into-upsc/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UPSC Civil Service posts overview</a> and official DoPT service descriptions. The Indian Forest Service (IFoS) is recruited through a separate exam, not through this CSE preference and rank process — see the FAQ below.
  </p>

  <h2 id="group-b">Group B services and the state route</h2>

  <p>
    A smaller share of vacancies each year go to Central Services, Group B — roles like Section Officer in the central secretariat, or Assistant Security Officer positions. These sit below Group A in seniority and pay scale but still offer a genuine central-government career with its own promotion path into Group A over time.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--gold">
    <p>
      Group B is not a consolation outcome, but it is a different kind of career: slower initial authority, a longer runway to Group A-equivalent responsibility, and generally less field exposure early on than IAS or IPS.
    </p>
    <p>
      Candidates who land here by rank should evaluate it on its own terms rather than treating it purely as a stepping stone.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    <strong>Whatever service or grade you land in, the skill portfolio you built while preparing keeps paying you back.</strong>
    Years of structured reading, current-affairs synthesis, essay writing, and interview communication are the same skills that carry weight in policy think tanks, regulatory affairs, public-policy consulting, and civil-society leadership roles. Aspirants who treat CSE prep purely as exam-cramming waste that transfer value; those who also build proof of work — a published policy note, a research paper, a documented case study — position themselves for a genuinely high-income opportunity even outside government, not only inside it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="later-career">Deputation, global bodies, and later-career moves</h2>

  <p>
    A civil-services career does not stay fixed to one department for 30 years. Deputation, national assignments, and post-retirement moves reshape most officers' paths well before retirement.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {laterCareerCards.map((item) => (
